---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Leaf from '../components/Leaf.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="About Under the Hazel Tree — who writes it, why it exists, and what you'll find here."
  art="banner-about"
  overlay
>
  <section class="banner">
    <img class="banner__art" src="/img/banner-about.svg" alt="" width="1600" height="560" fetchpriority="high" />
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Under the Hazel Tree</h1>
      <p>A small, slow corner of the internet for poems, stories, and quiet reflections.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--paper">
    <div class="shell shell--narrow">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          There is a hazel at the end of the garden. It was here before the house had my name on the deeds, and it will
          outlast the arrangement. Most of what's on this site was thought up standing under it with a cup of tea going
          cold.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is a personal site, in the older sense: no newsletter funnel, no course, nothing to sell. Poems on one
          shelf, stories on another, and a journal for the offcuts — the notes, the half-thoughts, the things that
          aren't finished and may never be.
        </p>

        <h2>Why the hazel</h2>

        <p>
          In the old stories, nine hazels leaned over the well at the source of the Boyne, and dropped their nuts into
          the water, and a salmon ate them and took on all the knowledge in the world. Whoever ate the salmon would have
          it in turn. In the event it went to a boy who burned his thumb on the cooking and put it in his mouth without
          thinking — which is, in my experience, exactly how knowledge arrives.
        </p>

        <p>
          Hazel is also the wood you cut for a walking stick, for a fence, for a diviner's rod. It coppices: cut it back
          hard and it comes again from the stool, better than before. That seemed like a reasonable thing to name a
          writing site after.
        </p>

        <h2>What's here</h2>

        <ul>
          <li><strong><a href="/poems/">Poems</a></strong> — short pieces, mostly about light, water and weather.</li>
          <li><strong><a href="/stories/">Stories</a></strong> — longer prose, memory-shaped.</li>
          <li><strong><a href="/journal/">Journal</a></strong> — working notes, reading, what's flowering.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Everything is written by one person and posted when it's ready, which is not a schedule. If you'd like it to
          come to you, there's an <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a>. If you'd rather be surprised, press
          <a href="/wander">wander</a> and the site will pick something for you.
        </p>

        <h2>Getting in touch</h2>

        <p>
          Corrections, arguments about hurling, and the names of things I've got wrong are all welcome. Replace this
          paragraph with your own address when you make the site your own.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="centred" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <a class="button" href="/poems/"><Leaf size={16} /> Start with the poems</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
